========================================================================
B7 — THE SCOPE OF ORIENTABILITY: time reversal is also an isometry
========================================================================
  S920 measured COORDINATE flows exp(sM) and their finite compositions
  = the connected component SO⁺(p,q). Question: does this exhaust the isometries?


  ⟹ [MACH] R is an isometry, and it swaps the cones FOR ANY q.
     Each cone is SEPARATELY invariant under SO⁺(p,q); the UNORDERED pair
     {C₊,C₋} is invariant under the whole of O(p,q).


  ⇒ ★★THE VISA'S CATCH WAS HERE — AND IT IS DEAD. I report it with its name attached, because a catch quietly withdrawn is a fudge.
     I wanted to say: "the rung-4 verdict silently narrowed the scope from isometries to the connected component". WRONG, for three reasons, each sufficient on its own:
     (1) the quantifier sits INSIDE the theorem itself, not in a qualifier below: "splits into two cones, INVARIANT WITH RESPECT TO ALL COORDINATE FLOWS". I read past it and attacked the sentence with an object its own quantifier excludes;
     (2) "orientABLE" — the suffix '-able' = POSSIBILITY (as in "solvable", "measurable"): orientable, not oriented. The language already carried what I was demanding be added;
     (3) the verdict says "an invariant sign of charge is POSSIBLE ⟺ …". "Possible" = a choice exists. My "correction" was a restatement of the sentence I was trying to fix.
     ⟹ B7 is not a catch, but an ADDENDUM TO THE QUALIFIER: the reflection R explicitly realizes the reversal of the cones. The content of rung-4 STANDS WHOLE.
  ★RESIDUE (hygiene, not a verdict): in §(2) of the court's text "an invariant
    sign of charge" stands BARE — the scope is inherited from §(3) below it, while
    §(2) is written in physical language. A sentence from §(2) lifted out of the
    court's text will have the reader supply the word "isometries" themselves.
    The cost of the fix is zero: write the scope into §(2) inline, as already done in §(1). This is a drift risk in citation, not a defect.

